 86: ## 5. Proof of concept (pre-fix)
 87: 
 88: Visual: open in an incognito window (logged out of everything), substituting the live base URL:
 89: 
 90: ```
 91: https://<cronometer-service>.up.railway.app/authorize?response_type=code&redirect_uri=https://example.com/cb&code_challenge=anything&code_challenge_method=S256
 92: ```
 93: 
 94: The approve page renders with no login prompt. This alone confirms §3.1.
 95: 
 96: Full mint (terminal; equivalent to any anonymous caller). Reuses the PKCE pair from the Basecamp review:
 97: 
 98: ```bash
 99: BASE="https://<cronometer-service>.up.railway.app"
100: VERIFIER="punchdrunk_pkce_test_verifier_0123456789abcdef"
101: CHALLENGE="M-8lrNM-WmPXoCaqdohdVHjfLwzUVv6cOPsQ54pEKyo"   # base64url(sha256(VERIFIER))
102: 
103: CODE=$(curl -s -i -X POST "$BASE/authorize" \
104:   -d "redirect_uri=https://example.com/cb" \
105:   -d "code_challenge=$CHALLENGE" \
106:   | grep -i '^location:' | sed -E 's/.*[?&]code=([^&]+).*/\1/' | tr -d '\r')
107: 
108: curl -s -X POST "$BASE/token" \
109:   -d "grant_type=authorization_code" -d "code=$CODE" -d "code_verifier=$VERIFIER"
110: ```
111: 
112: Pre-fix expected output: `{"access_token":"<MCP_AUTH_TOKEN>","token_type":"bearer","scope":"mcp"}`. **The returned token is live; do not paste it anywhere, and rotate it after testing.**
113: 
